enum CartItemAddAttemptStatus {
  PROCESSING
  COMPLETED
  REJECTED
}

// Phase 16A.0-DA, Unit DA.2 (see the DA.2 design review). Durable
// idempotency record for one client-supplied Idempotency-Key on
// POST /cart/items, preventing a retried request from incrementing
// CartItem.quantity twice. Scoped to addItem only - updateItemQuantity/
// removeItem are out of scope for this unit.
//
// Uniqueness is per (customerId, idempotencyKey), not global - this key is
// a client-minted per-action token, not a cross-account identifier (unlike
// CheckoutAttempt.idempotencyKey, which is globally unique). Two different
// customers may reuse the same key without collision.
//
// Lifecycle is a strict two-phase write, never a single transaction:
// Phase 1 (its own fast statement) creates this row at PROCESSING,
// attemptCount 1 - durably visible immediately, which is what lets a
// genuinely concurrent duplicate request observe ALREADY_PROCESSING
// instead of blocking on a Postgres row lock. Phase 2 (the addItem mutation
// transaction) mutates CartItem + CartReservationSyncState and transitions
// this row to COMPLETED atomically in the SAME transaction, fenced on
// (id, attemptCount, status='PROCESSING') - so there is never an
// observable state where the CartItem mutation committed but this row
// didn't reach COMPLETED, only the safe reverse (PROCESSING with no
// mutation applied - e.g. the server crashed between phase 1 and phase 2).
//
// REJECTED is distinct from a crash: it is written when phase 2's
// read-only business validation (product purchasable, quantity available)
// reaches a deterministic, typed rejection BEFORE any CartItem mutation is
// attempted - also fenced on attemptCount, via its own conditional update
// outside the mutation transaction (nothing to roll back). Infrastructure
// failures (DB/Redis/process errors) are never classified as REJECTED -
// they leave the row at PROCESSING, identical to a crash, recoverable only
// via stale reclaim.
//
// A PROCESSING row older than the staleness window (see
// CART_ITEM_ADD_ATTEMPT_STALE_TIMEOUT_MS) is reclaimed by incrementing
// attemptCount and re-executing phase 2 under the new count - never by
// deleting or re-creating the row, so its identity and audit trail survive
// exactly like every other permanent attempt/marker table in this schema.
model CartItemAddAttempt {
  id                    String                   @id @default(uuid())
  idempotencyKey        String
  customerId            String
  cartId                String
  productId             String
  requestedQuantity     Int
  status                CartItemAddAttemptStatus @default(PROCESSING)
  attemptCount          Int                      @default(1)
  rejectionCode         String?
  rejectionMessage      String?
  resultCartItemId      String?
  resultQuantity        Int?
  resultMutationVersion Int?
  resultGeneration      Int?
  createdAt             DateTime                 @default(now())
  updatedAt             DateTime                 @updatedAt

  customer User    @relation(fields: [customerId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  cart     Cart    @relation(fields: [cartId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  product  Product @relation(fields: [productId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  @@unique([customerId, idempotencyKey])
  @@index([createdAt])
  @@map("cart_item_add_attempts")
}

